title: Turned round twice
mode: draw
chapter: Signals and gates
check: behaves
parts: not
---- intro
A NOT gate turns its input round. What happens if you put **two** of them in a row?

The first turns `a` round. The second turns that round again. Fill in the table in your head before you try it:

| a | after one NOT | after two |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | ? |
| 1 | 0 | ? |
---- task
Wire `a` through both NOT gates, one after the other, into `y`.
---- start
input a 0 20
not n1 120 20
not n2 240 20
led y 380 20
---- reference
input a
led y
y = a
---- answer
input a 0 20
not n1 120 20
not n2 240 20
led y 380 20
a -> n1.in
n1 -> n2.in
n2 -> y
---- hint1
Three wires: switch to the first NOT, first NOT to the second, second NOT to the light.
---- hint2
Drag from the square on the right of each part to the square on the left of the next one.
---- solution
`a` into `n1`, `n1` into `n2`, `n2` into `y`. Two turns round bring you back where you started: `y` follows `a`.
---- done
Two NOTs cancel out. Real chips do use pairs like this, as a **buffer**: it does not change the value, but it makes the signal strong again after a long wire.
